---
import SealedSection from '../seal/SealedSection.astro';

const docBase = 'https://github.com/Vulnetix/belai/blob/main/docs/';

const sealText =
  'Web sessions: while the Vulnetix CLI is logged in, every Belai session mirrors to the Vulnetix website. Belai → Sessions follows a running session live and takes prompts that run on the host as if typed there. When the host stops to ask, the page shows the question and can answer it: a permission ask (allow once, allow always, deny), clarify questions, the mode choice and a plan review. Tool rows, diffs with syntax colour, reasoning and every role-manager and classifier decision show as the TUI draws them. The session JSONL is the single record; the website mirrors it, and a web answer is untrusted input applied through the host\'s own code. The first answer wins.';

const answers = [
  {
    kind: 'permission',
    choices: 'allow once · allow always · deny',
    detail:
      'The page shows the tool, its subject, the diff it would make and the exact rule allow-always adds. Allow-always writes that rule to the settings on the host, the same as pressing it there.',
  },
  {
    kind: 'question',
    choices: 'single or multiple choice · notes · skip · decline',
    detail:
      'The questions the model asked, with every option and its description. Your answers still pass the prompt classifier on the host, like typed ones.',
  },
  {
    kind: 'mode',
    choices: 'the options on the mode panel',
    detail: 'When a request reads like a different mode than the one you set, the host asks before switching. Answer it from the page.',
  },
  {
    kind: 'plan review',
    choices: 'approve here · approve in a new session · refine · keep planning',
    detail: 'Read the plan on the page and approve it, or send notes back. Notes take the web-prompt path, so they are never a slash or shell command.',
  },
];

const records = [
  ['turn_state', 'a turn started or ended, with the footer facts: mode, model, effort, branch, switches, context'],
  ['tool_start', 'a tool call started, so the page shows it running before its result lands'],
  ['tool · diff', 'what a tool changed on disk, as the rows the TUI drew'],
  ['ask', 'the question the host is waiting on, keyed by its ask id'],
  ['ask_answer', 'how it was resolved, and whether the host or the web answered'],
  ['rolemanager', 'every classifier and role-manager verdict, with its label, model and timing'],
];
---

<SealedSection label="web" sealText={sealText} id="web">
  <div class="prose-measure">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-vx-ink">Web sessions</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
      While the Vulnetix CLI is logged in, each
      session mirrors to the Vulnetix website. <strong>Belai → Sessions</strong> follows a running
      session live, and a prompt you type there runs on the host as if you had typed it at the
      terminal. When the host stops to ask you something, the page shows the question and you can
      answer it there. The host carries on as if you had pressed the key.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-start">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-vx-ink/30 p-5 mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-vx-ink/80" aria-label="A permission ask as the Sessions page shows it" role="img">
      <p><span class="text-vx-sand-amber">── permission</span> <span class="text-vx-ink/50">Edit ·············· waiting for an answer ──</span></p>
      <p class="mt-2 pl-4"><span class="text-vx-sand-amber">⌁</span> <span class="font-semibold text-vx-ink">Edit</span> internal/server/handler.go</p>
      <p class="pl-4 text-vx-ink/50">allow always adds Edit(internal/server/handler.go)</p>
      <div class="mt-2 ml-4 rounded bg-vx-ink/5">
        <p class="px-2"><span class="text-vx-ink/50"> 41</span>   ctx := r.Context()</p>
        <p class="px-2 bg-vx-coral/15"><span class="text-vx-coral">−42</span>   user := r.URL.Query().Get("user")</p>
        <p class="px-2 bg-vx-mint/20"><span class="text-vx-mint-strong">+42</span>   name := r.URL.Query().Get("user")</p>
        <p class="px-2 bg-vx-mint/20"><span class="text-vx-mint-strong">+43</span>   if name == "" {'{'}</p>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 pl-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="rounded border border-vx-mint-strong px-2 py-0.5 text-vx-mint-strong">Allow once</span>
        <span class="rounded border border-vx-ink/30 px-2 py-0.5">Allow always</span>
        <span class="rounded border border-vx-ink/30 px-2 py-0.5 text-vx-coral">Deny</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-measure">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-vx-ink">Drawn like the TUI</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-vx-ink/80">
        The page draws with the TUI's own design system: thread blocks with a top rule, quiet tool rows
        that only turn red when they fail, reasoning folded to one line, diffs with line numbers and
        syntax colour, the plan with <span class="mono">◆ ◇ ✓</span>, and the footer's mode, model,
        switches and context gauge. Amber still means the host needs you. Open questions are also
        pinned above the composer, so you can find them in a long transcript.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-vx-ink/80">
        Classifier and role-manager decisions appear in the transcript as they happen: what was
        checked, the verdict, the model that gave it and how long it took. Switch on{' '}
        <span class="mono">all decisions</span> to also see the ones the TUI folds into its own lines,
        like mode selection and goal evaluation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-vx-ink">What you can answer from the web</h3>
    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      {answers.map((a) => (
        <article class="rounded-lg border border-vx-ink/30 p-5">
          <h4 class="mono text-sm font-semibold text-vx-sand-amber">{a.kind}</h4>
          <p class="mono mt-1 text-xs text-vx-ink/60">{a.choices}</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-vx-ink/80">{a.detail}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="prose-measure mt-4 text-sm text-vx-ink/70">
      The agent picker and the first-run trust prompt stay on the host.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="prose-measure">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-vx-ink">The session file is the record</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        Belai writes everything to the session JSONL first. The website mirrors those lines, and a
        resumed session reads the same ones, so both show the same events:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1 text-sm text-vx-ink/80 list-none p-0">
        {records.map(([name, what]) => (
          <li><span class="mono font-semibold text-vx-ink">{name}</span> <span class="text-vx-ink/70">{what}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        Resume a session with <code class="mono">belai -r</code> and its diffs and answered questions
        are back on screen.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-measure">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-vx-ink">Web answers are checked on the host</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        The host applies an answer only to the question open right now, only if it fits that question
        (options that exist, one choice where only one is allowed, a known decision), and only through
        the same code its own keys call. Notes are cleaned of control characters and capped. The first
        answer wins: if you answer at the terminal first, the page shows that the host refused the late
        one and why.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        <code class="mono">sync.remote_answers</code> turns web answers off and{' '}
        <code class="mono">sync.remote_prompts</code> makes a session view-only. A repository's settings
        can turn either off, never on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10 prose-measure">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-vx-ink">Sync runs in the background</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
      The terminal never blocks on sync. A line is written to disk, and a background syncer uploads
      it about 30 ms later together with the lines that came with it. Large batches are gzipped. On
      the server, Postgres notifications wake the page's stream and the host's inbox as soon as
      something changes, so a line reaches the page, and your answer reaches the terminal, in about
      one round trip. History hides sessions run in <code class="mono">/tmp</code> until you ask for
      them. Read{' '}
      <a class="text-vx-ink underline" href={`${docBase}session-sync.md`}>docs/session-sync.md</a> for
      the details.
    </p>
  </div>
</SealedSection>
